== Parents Being Faithful to All under Their Care

=== §. 71. #emph[Of the duty of stepparents and parents-in-law.]

The extent of this title #emph[(fathers)] is the last thing to be #footnote[Those who are in the place of parents.] considered. Not only natural parents themselves, but also all who are in their place, are included under it. Those who are in the place of parents are so

+ By the bond of marriage.
+ By nearness of blood.
+ By voluntary appointment.

The first sort are stepparents and parents-in-law, who are counted so either by the marriage of the parents themselves or by the marriage of their children.

The man or woman who is married to a parent who had children before their marriage is in the place of a natural parent. Thus Joseph was a #footnote[Luke 2:48.] father to Jesus, and Keturah#footnote[Gen 25:1.] a mother to Isaac. So again the natural parents of a son who has married a wife, or of a daughter who is married to a husband, are in the place of parents to their son’s wife and to their daughter’s husband. Thus Jethro#footnote[Exod 18:1.] was a father to Moses, and Naomi#footnote[Ruth 3:1.] a mother to Ruth.

All these are to regard their stepchildren and children-in-law (that is, the #footnote[Stepparents and parents-in-law to perform the same duties as natural parents.] children of their husband or wife, or the wives of their sons and the husbands of their daughters) as their own natural children. They are to perform the duties named above according to the age and place of these children, and to seek their good in every way (except in such duties as belong in a distinctive way to natural parents, as #emph[nursing] to a natural mother, and #emph[leaving the inheritance] to a natural parent).

For a pattern of this, take the examples of Joseph and Naomi named above. What could natural parents do more for their own children than Joseph did for Jesus, and Naomi for Ruth? The history notes how Joseph took care to have his wife’s child #footnote[Luke 2:21, 22.] circumcised and presented in the temple; how he #footnote[Matt 2:14.] fled from place to place to preserve the child’s life; how #footnote[Luke 2:48.] distressed he was when he thought the child was lost; how he #footnote[#emph[v.] 51.] brought him up in his own house. The history also notes how Naomi#footnote[Ruth 1:22.] brought Ruth, her son’s wife, into her own country and kept her with herself, and #footnote[Ruth 2:22.] directed her where to go, and #footnote[Ruth 3:1.] what husband to take, and #footnote[Ruth 4:16.] became a nurse to her child.

1\. Marriage makes man and wife one flesh. In this respect #footnote[Reasons.] they ought to have one mind and the same feelings, as toward each other, so jointly toward each other’s children. On the same ground the husband or wife of a child, being one flesh with the child, ought to be respected as the child is. On this ground God counts the saints his #footnote[Eph 5:1 τέκνα ἀγαπητά.] “beloved children,” because they are #footnote[2 Cor 11:2.] betrothed to his natural Son.

2\. To respect the children of a husband or wife as one’s own is great evidence of entire love to that husband or wife. And to respect the husband or wife of a child is great evidence of love to the child itself. If the world’s proverb holds true #emph[(love me and love my dog),] how much more true is this Christian rule: #emph[love me and love my child,] or #emph[love me and love my husband,] or #emph[love me and love my wife.]

3\. This is also a special means of kindling and preserving mutual love between a man and wife who have children by former husbands and wives, and also between parents and children.

=== §. 72. #emph[Of the perverse conduct of stepparents and parents-in-law toward their children.]

Contrary to this is the conduct of most stepparents and parents-in-law, especially of those who are married to husbands or wives who had children before the marriage. They are so far from performing the duty named above that they rather envy the prosperity of their husbands’ and wives’ children, secretly try to hinder it all they can, and cunningly seek to turn the natural parent’s affection away from them. From this #footnote[Senecae Hippolytus.] fearful tragedies have been made, and lamentable harms have followed. What other reason can be given for this #footnote[#emph[Ovid. Metamorph. l.] 15. #emph[fab.] 45.] than a plain instigation of the devil, who labors in this way to divide those whom God has joined together? To avoid this snare, note the harms that follow from it.

1\. Such parents sin against God’s ordinance and, like Eve, lean more to Satan’s suggestion than to God’s direction.

2\. They estrange hearts and affections from one another: not only from their children, but from themselves.

3\. They provoke their stepchildren and children-in-law to scorn and despise them, and to do no duty to them.

From this, note into what a hell unkind stepparents and parents-in-law cast themselves. If this were duly weighed, I think they would be of another mind. But the god of this world so blinds their eyes with self-love and envy that they cannot see the harms into which they plunge themselves.

This point is all the more to be weighed because the fault rebuked here is so common, and has been all too common in every age. #footnote[#emph[Lurida terribiles miscent aconita nouercae.] \[Fearsome stepmothers mix deadly aconite.\] #emph[Ouid. Metam. l.] 1. #emph[fab.] 4.] What grievous complaints have been made in former times, and are still made, by children against stepparents! From this, dreadful curses have also followed. Let widowers and widows who have children think of it seriously beforehand, and be all the more careful in taking a second or third husband or wife. And after they are married, let them beware of Satan’s snares, and let conscience of duty prevail with them more than the corruption of nature.

=== §. 73. #emph[Of the faults of parents toward their children’s husbands and wives.]

Many parents also greatly transgress and swerve from their duty toward the husbands and wives of their children. For

1\. They will show much more respect to their own children than to their children’s husbands or wives: as by coming to their daughters when their husbands are away from home, and never except then, and by sending for their sons to their house, but not for their sons’ wives.

2\. In all disagreements they will take their own children’s side, even when their children are in the wrong, and show great partiality.

3\. They will often give very bad advice: advising their sons to keep their wives on a short allowance, and advising their daughters not to be too subject to their husbands, and even to steal secretly from their husbands.

4\. If they live with their children, they will pry so into everything their child’s husband or wife does, and show such suspicion and distrust in everything, that they cannot help causing much discord. So it often comes about that either parent and child, or husband and wife, must be parted: they cannot all live together in peace.

I note these faults in this way so that they may be avoided the more carefully.

=== §. 74. #emph[Of the duty of those who are in place of parents to orphans.]

The next sort of those who are in place of parents are those who are next to the parents in blood and kindred, such as grandfathers, grandmothers, uncles, aunts, elder brothers or sisters, their husbands or wives, and other kinsmen.

These are commonly counted to be in the place of parents #footnote[The next of kin to orphans to be as natural parents to them.] when God takes away natural and immediate parents from their children, so that they are parents to orphans.

The duties which were prescribed to natural parents belong to them (except those #footnote[§. 71.] excepted before). Their duty is to see these orphans well educated, well placed in some good calling, well married, and (if the orphans are left without means) well provided for.

Mordecai is a worthy precedent and pattern for this kind of parent. “He had brought up … Esther, the daughter of his uncle, because she did not have a father or mother,”#footnote[Esth 2:7.] and he took her in as his own daughter. When she was taken from him, he went “to learn about Esther’s welfare and what was happening to her,”#footnote[#emph[v.] 11.] and #footnote[#emph[v.] 20, 22. #emph[Chapter] 4. #emph[v.] 13.] he gave her advice from time to time on how to conduct herself. Let those who are parents to orphans take this example as a direction, and as a motive to perform the other duties prescribed to natural parents as need requires and occasion is offered.

When God by his providence takes away natural parents, he commits their orphans as a charge to the next of kin, so that as their natural parents brought them into the world, these should bring them up. Therefore let them look to God for their recompense (who takes it on himself to be #footnote[Ps 68:5.] “a father of the fatherless”), and for their encouragement let them remember the reward which Mordecai received from the Lord. Indeed, let them also remember the blessing that God bestowed on Esther. God often so blesses the means used for the education of orphans that those who take the care of it have no cause to regret their pains and cost, but rather have much cause for comfort, joy and thanksgiving.

=== §. 75. #emph[Of the common neglect of orphans.]

All too common is the contrary disposition of most kinsmen and kinswomen. They think they are bound to none but their own children, and if they have no children, to none at all. Many are of the mind of Ruth’s kinsman: they fear they would “jeopardize” #footnote[Ruth 4:6.] their own inheritance. Though they are themselves exceedingly rich, and spend much on clothing, pastimes and their households, yet they can allow the orphans of their poor kindred to go without, and to beg out of need.

This is what makes the loss of natural parents so much the greater. And in this many Christians are more inhuman than the heathen. The apostle implies as much where he says that they are “worse than an unbeliever”;#footnote[1 Tim 5:8.] for orphans who are next of kin are to be counted among those whom the apostle calls a man’s #footnote[τοὺς ἰδίους.] OWN.

=== §. 76. #emph[Of the duty of guardians.]

The last sort of those who are in place of parents are those who have the charge of children committed to them by the voluntary appointment of parents, or who are chosen by the children themselves, or by the magistrate when the parents are dead, or lunatic, or in any other way incapable and unable to train up their own children.

From these, care and conscience are required rather than cost and expense; for commonly their expenses are allowed them and their pains are repaid.

Those who have the charge of children committed to them because of the death or incapacity of the natural parents are commonly called #emph[guardians,] and two general duties are required of them.

+ That they see to their wards’ good education.
+ That they preserve their patrimony and inheritance.

As for #emph[their education,] they are to observe the directions given before to natural parents.

As for #emph[preserving their patrimony,] they must do for their wards what they would do for their own children, and so conduct themselves that they may with a good conscience give their wards, or anyone else, the account which the law requires.

These two duties are the main ends for which guardians are chosen. If they are faithful in performing them, great amends will be made for the untimely death of parents. Though parents die before their children are old enough to manage their estate, yet their houses and families will be maintained, and young heirs kept from swindlers and cheats.

It is noted of John, who had the mother of Jesus entrusted #footnote[John 19:27.] to him, that he “took her into his home.” This implies his provident care for her good.

=== §. 77. #emph[Of the fraud of guardians.]

Contrary to this is the practice of most guardians, who take wards #footnote[#emph[Tutela ad vtilitatem eorum qui commissi sunt, non ad eorum quibus commissa, gerenda est. Cic. Offic. l.] 1.] for their own advantage and make a prey of them. The heathen could say that guardianship is to be carried out for the benefit of the ward, not of the guardian. Yet among Christians this fault of guardians is found.

1\. There are few children (who do not have natural parents to see to their good education) who are not greatly neglected in it, and brought up very untaught and unrestrained. Indeed, many times they are kept too sparingly and stingily in food, clothing and other needful things, though they have enough to pay for all that will be spent on them.

2\. The main estate and patrimony of many wards is much diminished. If they have lands, as much waste is made of them as possibly can be. And when an account is to be given, it will be an inflated, unjust and most unconscionable account. So long as the guardian can get a general release, that is all he cares about.

3\. Marriages are in a way forced on many wards, and such matches put on them as they cannot like; indeed, such as are in no way fit for them, neither in standing, nor years, nor build of body, nor qualities of mind.

Little do these guardians think of the account to which they will be brought one day before the Lord, who calls himself the “helper of the fatherless,”#footnote[Ps 10:14.] and who sees the wrong done, to repay it. Fearful is the judgment which the Lord has #footnote[Exod 22:22, #emph[etc.]] threatened against those who oppress the fatherless. Therefore let unjust guardians not think that the gain they get wrongfully from their wards will prosper; rather let them expect God’s curse on their own children and goods.

If those to whom parents themselves have committed their children on their deathbeds, trusting in their faithfulness, deal deceitfully with their wards, their sin is doubled and tripled. For

+ They violate the bond of friendship, faithfulness and the trust placed in them.
+ They sin directly against justice.
+ They most monstrously transgress the rules of love.

=== §. 78. #emph[Of the duty of schoolmasters and tutors.]

Among those who are set in the place of parents by voluntary appointment, #emph[schoolmasters] and #emph[tutors] are to be counted. The authority and charge of a parent is committed to them for a time; therefore for that time they ought to perform a parent’s duty.

Their duty is confined to education. It does not belong to them to maintain the children committed to them; rather, they themselves are maintained by their pains in that work.

All the duties which were included before under #emph[nurture] belong to #emph[schoolmasters] and #emph[tutors.] More particularly, they are to instruct children in three things.

+ In learning.
+ In civility and good manners.
+ In true piety and religion.

I need not speak of these points more than has #footnote[§. 28, 34.] been delivered before. Only, to stir them up to be diligent in nurturing children well in those three points, let the benefit of good schoolmasters and tutors be well noted.

Schoolmasters commonly cast the first seed into the hearts of #footnote[The benefit of a good schoolmaster.] children; they are the first to season them. As the seed is, so will the crop be; as the first seasoning is, so will the savor continue to be. If they sow no good seed at all, what harvest can be expected? If they sow a corrupt seed of rudeness, license, profaneness, superstition, or any wickedness, the crop must of necessity correspond to it. But if they sow the good seed of learning, civility and piety, there is great hope of a good crop.

From this it follows that they are of great use, and may bring much good, not only to the children themselves and their parents, #footnote[#emph[Scholas & phrontisteria virorum & mulierum philosophiae ethnicae addictorum construxit.] \[He built schools and places of study of men and women devoted to heathen philosophy.\] #emph[Nicep. eccl. hist. l.] 10. #emph[c.] 21. The benefit of a good tutor.] but also to the church and commonwealth. Julian the Apostate was not ignorant of this. Therefore, in his endeavor to root Christianity out of the world, he allowed no man who professed himself a Christian to teach a school, but in place of Christian schoolmasters he set up heathen schoolmasters in every part of his dominions.

Tutors, to whose government young students sent to the universities are committed, have to deal with children in their riper years, at the very time when they are to be settled in a course of life: the very time in which much good may be done to children, or else in which they may be utterly corrupted. There will be little hope of bending them and drawing them into another course after they are freed from a tutor’s government. Tutors therefore, as they need great wisdom, so they need a good conscience, so that they may take the greater care of their pupils and the greater pains in teaching them the three points named above: #emph[learning, civility,] and #emph[piety.] A good tutor may do much to repair the negligence and amend the defects of a schoolmaster; but there is no one left to redress the failings of a tutor. Children are for the most part past remedy when they cease to have a tutor.

=== §. 79. #emph[Of the negligence of schoolmasters and tutors.]

The vices contrary to the duty of schoolmasters named above are these.

1\. #emph[Unskillfulness.] Many who are fitter to be taught than to teach take this weighty calling on themselves. Some lack sufficient learning; some again are rude and do not themselves know good manners; and some do not know the first principles of the oracles of God. No good can be expected from these.

2\. #emph[Covetousness.] All that many seek is to multiply the number of their pupils and to get as much as they can for boarding and teaching. They aim more at their own gain than at their pupils’ profit. Children are not likely to thrive well under them.

3\. #emph[Negligence.] Many are unwilling to take the pains of a schoolmaster, and so they come into the school as little as they can, take every small occasion to be gone again, and do not care how much their children play. If they can hire a poor assistant teacher, all the burden is thrown on him. Little fruit can be expected from them.

4\. #emph[Lack of piety.] Many skillful and painstaking schoolmasters, under whose teaching children come on exceedingly well in learning, think that it does not belong to them at all to teach the grounds of religion, so that they wholly neglect piety. Surely they lack it themselves; for if their own hearts were seasoned with true piety, their consciences would move them to teach it to their children. The things of the kingdom of God are like #emph[leaven,] which seasons the whole batch of dough with the seasoning and savor it has itself. When children spend almost all their time with their schoolmasters, from whom should they learn it, if not from them? What blessing can be hoped for from the pains of such schoolmasters?

To these I might add the excessive laxness of some (by which children grow too headstrong) and the too great severity of others, either in correcting too often, as if they delighted in it, or in the measure of it, as if they were dealing with beasts; by this they frighten children and make them dull, and impudent too. But these extremes were #footnote[§. 48, 49.] handled before.

The common fault of tutors is to neglect their pupils altogether. Many think that being a tutor is a matter of mere title: that no more is required of them than to bear the name, and to guarantee that the college is paid for their pupils’ board. This notion, and the practice that goes with it, is the blemish and bane of the universities. Many children well trained up in schools utterly lose the benefit of all their former education when they are sent to the university, because their tutors leave them wholly to themselves, and so they are made a prey to idle and wicked companions. Because of this many parents are utterly discouraged from sending their children to the university.
